  description="Windows, entirely, plus a short list of deliberate cuts and open design questions: sourced from docs/specs/deferred.md."
  crumb="Reference / What's not built"
  activeSlug="not-built"
>
  <article>
    <h1>What's not built yet</h1>
    <ReferencePills slug="not-built" />
    <p class="lede">
      shep is feature-complete for v1 on macOS and Linux: every verb in the
      CLI reference, the whistle MCP server, all four lookout panes, dogs,
      serve/dev/runtime, and config and packaging. This page is the honest
      remainder: one platform, and a short list of smaller cuts, each with
      the reason it isn't here yet.
    </p>

    <h2>Windows: the whole tier, not partial</h2>
    <p>
      Every command prints <code>shep does not yet support Windows</code> and
      exits <code>1</code>. This isn't a gap in an otherwise-working port:
      no Windows code path exists to be partial. WSL2 covers the common case
      today.
    </p>
    <Callout variant="careful">
      This was a costed decision, not an indefinite punt. The earlier
      estimate ("roughly +30-40% on the daemon's process-control layer")
      was, in its own words, mostly guesswork. A real survey replaced it on
      2026-08-15: <strong>roughly 36-49 tasks across 4-5 phases, and a
      redesign wearing a port's clothes</strong>, not a port on its own. Rin
      ruled Windows out of v1 once that number existed to act on, rather than
      leaving it last on a list behind a guess.
    </Callout>
    <p>
      Most of the surface is mechanical: 145 <code>cfg(unix)</code> sites,
      and un-gating them is close to free, since four whole module trees
      (<code>commands</code>, <code>dog</code>, <code>lookout</code>,
      <code>whistle</code>) turn out to contain no Unix API calls at all. The
      cost concentrates in a handful of places where the
      <strong>behaviour</strong>, not just the API, has no Windows analogue:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Graceful stop has no equivalent.</strong>
        <code>CTRL_BREAK_EVENT</code> reaches only console apps carrying
        their own handler, so <code>shep stop</code> would degrade to
        <code>shep kill</code> for anything that didn't opt into the
        shepherd channel.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>The shepherd channel can't be fd 3.</strong>
        <code>cmd.exe</code> has no fd-3 redirection, so the channel would
        need to become a named pipe named by an environment variable instead:
        the wire format survives, the transport doesn't.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong><code>user</code>/<code>group</code> would refuse
        permanently.</strong> Dropping privilege on Windows needs a logon
        session or a primary-token privilege: a different feature, not a
        different call.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p class="fine">
      Also on the table and not yet decided: whether permanent non-support is
      the right long-term answer. A tier isn't built once, it's maintained
      forever on a platform nobody on this project runs day to day.
    </p>

    <h2>Five smaller cuts, argued for rather than just missing</h2>
    <p>
      Spec §2 names these as deliberate v1.1+ scope, not oversights:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>HTTP/SSE transport for whistle.</strong> Ships stdio-only
        first; an AI agent reaches it the way any MCP stdio server is
        reached today.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>cgroup v2 enforcement</strong> (<code>enforce =
        "kernel"</code>). The polling <code>LimitEnforcer</code> is the v1
        tier; a kernel-enforced ceiling is later work.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>The <code>@shep/io</code> npm shim.</strong> Built on
        demand, not before anyone's asked for it.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>VCS metadata</strong> (the <code>vcs</code> feature). Off by
        default, not wired into the build.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>A JSON status endpoint.</strong> Resolved rather than just
        deferred: the metrics dog serves Prometheus exposition text for a
        scraper, which turned out to be an incompatible shape for what would
        have been a hand-fetched dashboard payload. That's a different
        feature, not a narrower version of this one, so it stays its own
        open item rather than folding into the dog.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Scheduled, not decided against</h2>
    <p>
      Named as v1.0 scope in the spec and hasn't reached the front of the
      queue. Lookout's three items in this section shipped in Phase 16
      (search and filter, the action keys behind the control gate, and
      lambs in the detail pane), which leaves one:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>OTLP export for the metrics dog.</strong> The dog serves
        Prometheus exposition only today; no <code>otel</code> cargo feature
        exists yet.
      </li>
    </ul>
